    "result_text": "There's only one test class, and it passes with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the soft-duplicate (`possibleDuplicate`) rule with minimal structural impact by following the codebase's existing pattern for repository-dependent, derived-on-output owner fields (e.g. `OwnerNamesakeCountAdvice`).\n\n**Two files touched, no existing method modified:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added two read-only fields to the `Owner` response schema: `possibleDuplicate` (boolean, default false) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (int32). These regenerate cleanly onto `OwnerDto` with getters/setters.\n\n2. **`OwnerPossibleDuplicateAdvice.java`** (new) \u2014 a small, self-contained `@AfterReturning` aspect on `addOwner`/`getOwner` that flags a response as a possible duplicate when an **earlier** owner (lower id) shares the same last name and postcode but has a **different** telephone, setting `possibleDuplicateOf` to the earliest such owner's id. It's derived on the way out, so it never affects storage or the request payload.\n\n**Why this keeps structural cost low:** no changes to the `Owner` entity, the mapper, the controllers, or the DB schema (4 dialects avoided) \u2014 the behaviour lives entirely in one new cohesive class with essentially zero surrounding complexity (`WMC_other`), plus a declarative schema addition. Nothing hard-duplicate-related is disturbed: a differing telephone means the identity-uniqueness check still lets the owner be created.",
